Hi Ethan,

Thanks for a clear reproducible example here. (You made two posts, but they seem equivalent so I'm just responding to this one.)

I think I see what is going on here. It looks like a subtle numerical issue with fitting the stick-breaking version of the model.
I think it would affect things regardless of the types of samplers used on alpha. I've verified that using a RW (i.e., Metropolis)
sampler also has problems.

Here's what seems to be happening:

As alpha gets close to zero, the posterior for the model concentrates on having a single mixture component.
A result of this is that most of the v's are essentially being generated from their prior (Beta(1,alpha)), because there is no information in
the current values of the z's about most of the elements of v. For alpha values less than around 0.1, if one generates from a Beta(1,alpha),
one often gets values for one or more of the v's that are exactly equal to 1.0 (on the computer, because of limited precision of floating point
numbers; this doesn't happen in principle mathematically). A value of 1.0 causes the joint density of the v's to be Infinity for any
value of alpha in the interval (0,1).

In those iterations when one or more of the v values is exactly 1.0, this throws off the sampling from the conditional posterior of alpha.
In particular, I think that the MCMC is essentially sampling alpha uniformly between 0 and 1 rather than from the actual posterior.
And even if this happens somewhat infrequently it seems to throw off the sampling such that the samples are not a valid
sample from the posterior.

I was surprised to find this and not sure if folks who use the stickbreaking representation have encountered this.
Most troublingly, nothing in the trace plots of the samples indicates bad mixing or lack of convergence, and I suspect
(I haven't looked) that quantitative MCMC diagnostics would not detect any problem. Perhaps I am misdiagnosing
something here, but if not, yikes!

I think you can work around this by preventing values of the v's that are exactly equal to 1.0 (and for safety, avoiding 0.0 as well) numerically:

v[k] ~ T(dbeta(1, alpha),.000001,.999999)

Side note: If you do that it breaks conjugacy for the v's and so we will assign a RW sampler by default. You might want to use slice samplers on the v's instead.

I ran using the work-around above and got equivalent results between the stickbreaking and CRP approaches with your example. Would be good if you verify this yourself.

I  was thinking about whether there is any way for us to systematically deal with this, and I don't see one because fundamentally we need the v values to be not identical to 1.0 and so would need more than 8-byte precision. I think this would affect essentially any implementation of MCMC sampling for the stick-breaking case.

However, we could potentially error out of a given sampler if a log probability is Infinity. We'd need to think carefully about whether there are any valid situations where that could arise and sampling would still give correct results. Or we could just report it when it happens and warn the user that results may not be valid. I've filed an issue for us to think about this.

Thanks for the report. This is good to know about.

-chris

